[Applause]  for a game so concerned with the perils  of futurism it's ironic that prey feels  like it's trapped in the past  it takes inspiration from immersive Sims  like System Shock deus ex and Bioshock  but while these titles take ideas  underpinning the genre and push them  forward  praise humdrum execution of the  fundamentals leave it feeling stuck in  time set in the year 2025 prey cosplays  as Morgan you a scientist that finds  herself or himself trapped on tellus won  a space station overrun by a race of  highly intelligent aliens called the  typhoon  praise opening is its most memorable  part largely due to its own would you  kindly moment and the promise of what it  could mean  praise early game twist lays the  groundwork for players to scrutinize  their agency question the motivations of  characters and reevaluate the impact  they're having it's an opportunity to  tell a twisting paranoia filled story  that forces you to second-guess your own  character but that opportunity is  largely wasted prey quickly loosens its  grip on this narrative thread allowing  you to drift into the background in  favor of environmental storytelling that  shifts the focus to tell us one itself  with its fusion of art deco stylings and  practical design tell us one is  initially very striking but it's  consistent aesthetic throughout is  broken only by one standout area  otherwise the place is comprised of  typical living cores  office areas and science labs logically  I can appreciate that the lack of  variety makes sense it's an institution  designed to house people that do science  not a funfair nevertheless the lack of  variety doesn't provide much incentive  to stop and admire surroundings beyond  the initial few hours good immersive  Sims like Bioshock and designed weave  stories into their environments telling  micro stories in the process but in prey  those stories are limited to the  uninteresting a spacestation ruined by a  catastrophic event and the hubris of its  leaders is evocative in itself but pray  never colors in the outline of these  events making the world feel like it  lacks depth smaller tales involving the  people stationed on tellus one can be  found by reading emails at computer  terminals and although they provide a  little more to latch on to by and large  is all fairly forgettable there's the  occasional meaningful exchange but the  abundance of mundane knows between  colleagues makes reading them a chore  and overall they feel like filler for an  overarching narrative that stretched  thin a key part of prey story involves  presenting conflicting evidence about  Morgan's personally before the memory  loss specifically her intent for tellus  one and her contingency for a typhoon  outbreak the game wants you to define  your Morgan but only serves up a handful  of moments to invite you to do so these  come in the form of uninspired missions  for characters who come off as  distractions from the main narrative  instead of pivotal figures in the story  he makes an honest effort of raising the  profile of these people by app and is  late in the game and when all said and  done the characters didn't really speak  to me there's a restraint to pray that  creates a disquieting quality in tell us  one but when this philosophy is extended  to his characters it just makes them  fade away  can be blamed for thinking about  ignoring a guy that wants you to go out  of your way to fetch a personal I effect  or a lady who asks you to spend precious  resources in battling typhoon to grab  her some medicine it turns out however  these menial tasks are critical to the  ending and when the game later the  unexpected way it's all tied together  the revelation didn't feel earned pray  does occasionally deliver an engagement  mission but they're few and far in  between  praise gameplay experience fares better  but it's uneven and at times the systems  feel at odds with themselves with  limited access to weapons and special  abilities much of the early game feels  like a slog it's like the worst parts of  a survival horror a punishing war of  attrition but without the cycle of  tension and release that makes it  enjoyable this becomes more pronounced  when you consider that mimics the most  prevalent type of typhoon enemy in the  early stages have a tendency of  appearing in blind spots and catching  you off god this aspect of the game is  both exhilarating and infuriating the  mimics are small highly mobile creatures  that can shape-shift into innocuous  objects around them all this was  effective in creating tension I felt  they also detracted from exploration I  wasn't soaking in the atmosphere as much  as I was painstakingly scouring it to  find that mimic before it chips away at  my health and when I took on the mimics  the lethargic controls of gunplay  coupled with the small black spider  darting around the floor dimly lit room  and leaping off walls made me feel more  like mr. bean I also felt forced to play  conservatively for most of the game the  scarcity of health packs and ammunition  meant it was in my best interest to  sneak by enemies which goes against  preys claim that you can play your way  and became frustrating when enemies  swarmed around objectives as the game  progresses however neuro mods become  more abundant which in turn means that  you can unlock abilities that level the  playing field somewhat this is one  praised combat opens up and while it  doesn't provide as much room for  creativity as Dishonored 2 weapon and  abili combinations develop a satisfying  synergy some powers allow you to take  control of enemies to fight alongside  you for a short period of time or  teleport you short distances to get the  jump on enemies as new foals are  introduced it becomes imperative to use  the psycho scope a helmet with a scanner  on it to research the typhoon and reveal  their individual weaknesses while also  unearthing more ability to unlock the  downside of using alien powers is that  turrets littered around talisman  identify you as being part - and open  fire on you again early on this feels  like punishment for trying to explore  the more interesting wrinkles of combat  but with time tourists also become less  of an issue as they can be hacked or  easily destroyed  in its later stages praise combat feels  varied and strategic you also gain the  ability to break down and reassemble  junk into useful items ranging from  weaponry and neuro mod upgrades to  turrets and med packs having this  ability ease the lack of resources and  empowered me to really approach combat  and exploration how I wanted I could go  into any scenario feeling like I had a  decent shot of defeating the - and  achieving my objective neuro mods can  also be used to solve preys puzzles  which mostly involve figuring out how to  gain entry into inaccessible locations  while not challenging these puzzles  shine a spotlight on the layout of Talas  by allowing you a degree of freedom in  how you approach them on a small scale  looping vents multi-layered rooms and  gravity lifts that act as arteries  throughout the station show  consideration has been given to the  physical construction of Telus 1 the  glue cannon is particularly effective in  showing how praised environments can  fold in on themselves it's phone can be  launched at walls and when it hardens  Morgan can use it as a makeshift  platform this makes it possible to  circumvent the obvious path in favour of  a more diverse approach highlighting the  thoughtful level design  unfortunately I encountered a number of  technical issues with pray in my  playthrough the most severe of which  prevented quest progression entirely  these had to be resolved by loading and  earlier safe which meant losing some  progress pray also suffers from lengthy  load times when moving between areas and  characters frequently clip through walls  resulting in an overall experience that  feels rough  [Music]  prey is a game of uneven pacing and  uninteresting characters it opens with a  poignant thought-provoking premises but  fails to follow through until the end  when it claims a revelation it doesn't  quite earn its gameplay follows out of  the gate but eventually matures into  something worthwhile if a bit familiar  as an homage to System Shock its  competent and at times even enjoyable  however prey fails to distinguish itself  and next to immersive sim contemporaries  such as Dishonored it feels stagnant  [Music]  you

